    90	
    91	The session runs at `high`: the talk, the build, the fixes, the tests. Plan, design and architecture run
    92	at `max` <!-- CEO-OK: plan-two-writers-sol-audit-2026-10-19 -->. **He switches with /effort; the lead warns him — on every reply, until he has.**
    93	
    94	- **The warning comes before the thinking** <!-- CEO-OK: plan-warning-before-the-thinking-2026-10-12 -->.
    95	  The moment his message turns the talk to a plan, a design or an architecture — to plan, propose or
    96	  evaluate one, not to ask a plain fact about what exists — the lead runs
    97	  `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py plan` before it thinks about the substance; it answers with the
    98	  live level and whether this reply opens with `⚠ Muhittin Bey, plan konuşmasındayız — /effort max'a geçin.`
    99	  (not when he is already at max). Until he has switched, that reply does no plan, design or architecture
   100	  thinking at all: the line, the plain facts he asked in a line or two, the wait, and no file. An
   101	  architecture weighed at high and warned about at the end is the failure this exists for (2026-10-12). His
   102	  plan permission mode starts the same warning by itself.
   103	- In the reply that brings him the plan for his yes, the lead runs
   104	  `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py build` and closes that reply with
   105	  `⚠ Muhittin Bey, plan bitti — koda geçmeden /effort high'a geçin.` — the warning travels with the
   106	  question, so his yes can arrive at high and the build starts at once. A yes that arrives at another
   107	  level gets the line again, and nothing is written until he has switched; a correction instead of a yes
   108	  → `plan` again. At the job's end: `python3 .claude/hooks/dxb-effort-warn.py off`.
   109	- Between those, the hook `dxb-effort-warn.py` repeats the line on each of his messages until the
   110	  session's live level matches. With no mode set, a message of his naming a plan, a design or an
   111	  architecture tells the lead to decide first, before the thinking, and — when the reply would be plan work —
   112	  to run `plan`, open with the max line and stop; and a session whose level is not high reminds the lead to
   113	  open any reply in which it writes code itself with `⚠ Muhittin Bey, koda geçmeden /effort high'a geçin.`
   114	  — a small job is not written at max either. Outside a plan, code a medium `helper-writer` writes needs
   115	  no switch. After a plan the session returns to high in every case, whoever writes: the lead's own turns
   116	  — specifying, verifying, testing — belong at high, not at max.
   117	- Measured 2026-10-06: neither a hook's input nor its environment carries the effort (`CLAUDE_EFFORT`
   118	  reaches the Bash tool, not a UserPromptSubmit hook). The live level is the newest, by timestamp, of the
   119	  status line's record (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/claude-ctx/<session>.json`, field `effort`), this session's last
   120	  step in the transcript, and its last /effort row there (`Set effort level to …`, written by a switch
   121	  between turns; a switch in the middle of a turn writes none) — none known → an unknown level.
   122	  A skill's `effort` can raise a turn but never lower one — the reason the old design-at-max skill went.
   123	
   124	## 3. The seats and the arrangement
   125	
   126	| Seat | Who | Does | Never |
   127	|---|---|---|---|
   128	| **Lead** | the session, `high` | chooses the hands, writes what it keeps, verifies every piece and every fix, runs the tests, commits, tells him | approves its own work; commits a piece it has not verified; asks him a technical question |
   129	| **Fork** | the lead's own copy (`subagent_type: "fork"`), its whole context | writes a piece that needs this conversation <!-- CEO-OK: fork-seat-no-size-2026-10-08 --> | commits |
   130	| **Writing helper** | `helper-writer` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`) | writes and tests a piece the lead specified; fixes a Sol finding | commits; widens the piece <!-- CEO-OK: helpers-write-code-under-lead-verification-2026-10-03 --> |
   131	| **Helper** | `helper` (Opus 5.5 · `medium`, read-only) | reads code or documents, measures, researches | writes |
   132	| **Reader** | a one-shot `scout` or `Explore` | a wide search whose text would swell the lead's context | writes; is resumed (its cache dies at 5 min) |
   133	| **Escalation writer** | `builder` (Opus 5.5 · `max`), one-shot | only a piece that failed twice at `high` | is opened for a routine fix |
   134	| **Second writer** | Fable 5.1 · `max`, its own process through `scripts/governance/second-plan.sh` <!-- CEO-OK: plan-two-writers-sol-audit-2026-10-19 --> | writes the same plan from the same brief, unseen (§1) | sees the lead's draft; audits; writes code |
   135	| **Auditor** | GPT-6.1 Sol <!-- CEO-OK: auditor-sol-6-1-2026-10-01 -->, through `scripts/governance/refuter.sh` | one blind pass over the job's commits (§4); on a plan job, also the blind plan audit (§1) | writes; audits the same thing twice; sees the lead's reasoning or verdict |
   136	
   137	Fable 5.1 is not consulted in a dxb-team2 job — neither the `advisor` tool (its own "before the work
   138	and before done" advice does not apply here) nor the Fable-based `design-eye`; its one seat is the plan's
   139	second writer, which writes and never advises.
   140	
   141	**The arrangement — four, all equal**
   142	<!-- CEO-OK: orchestration-three-arrangements-lead-chooses-2026-10-03 --> <!-- CEO-OK: four-equal-arrangements-lead-chooses-2026-10-08 -->:
   143	
   144	| Arrangement | Who writes |
   145	|---|---|
   146	| **Lead** | the lead itself |
   147	| **Fork** | the fork, while the lead stays free to verify |
   148	| **Team** | `helper-writer`s at `medium`, each its own piece, in parallel; `helper`s read and research beside them; the lead at `high` directs them |
   149	| **Hybrid** | the fork writes the core; `helper-writer`s the side pieces; `helper`s read |
   150	
   151	After the plan is talked through at `max` and he says yes, the lead at `high` looks at the job and chooses
   152	one of the four by its own judgement, and tells him in one line which and why. Writing itself is one of the
   153	four, not the default.
   154	
   155	- Parallel writers never touch the same file, and run their tests with `DXB_ENGINE_LOCK_WAIT=200`: one
   156	  run at a time holds the construction engine, and with it a second one queues instead of being refused
   157	  — inside the 4-minute rule (§7), so the lead starts no battery while writers are testing.
   158	- Each writer gets a spec it can work from alone: the files, the
   159	  behaviour, the measured facts it must not re-litigate, the test it writes first, what it reports.
   160	- Whoever writes — lead, fork, `helper-writer`, `builder` — writes by the principles in
   161	  `references/engineering-craft/codebase-design.md` and, before hand-over, checks only its own lines once
   162	  against its 12 smells: fixed there, or named with a reason. No extra agent, no extra round; the lead
   163	  never sends work back on a smell <!-- CEO-OK: ye18-pocock-skills-trimmed-for-both-2026-10-19 -->.
   164	- The lead verifies each piece before it is committed — reads the diff, runs its tests — and commits
   165	  only what it verified. Nobody else commits.
   166	- A piece that fails: a medium writer's goes to the lead or a fork at `high`; one that failed twice at
   167	  `high` goes to the `builder`, never earlier. Opus `xhigh` is never used for code (FrontierCode 51.4 %, the bottom of the
   168	  curve); `max` writes code only as the escalation writer — and for eye-facing code, the look of a page or a screen,
   169	  which is written at `max` <!-- CEO-OK: eye-facing-work-xhigh-max-studio-skills-2026-10-28 -->.
   170	
   171	## 4. Sol — one blind pass
   172	
   173	Every job that changes code, a rule or a door gets one Sol pass, after BUILD — the light lane (§1) excepted; a change to the records
   174	alone (STATE, the board) is checked by the rulers on commit instead.
   175	
   176	- **Launch:** `scripts/governance/refuter.sh [--effort medium|high|xhigh] "<brief>"` — `high` by
   177	  default; `xhigh` for money, a database, security, approval, governance or the agents; `medium` for a
   178	  text-only change. An audit can outlast the Bash tool's 10 minutes: launch it detached (a small
   179	  script under `setsid nohup`, its output to the job folder, `EXIT=` on its last line) and wait for
   180	  that line.
   181	- **The brief — blind, not in the dark:** his words verbatim; the plan's decisions as facts ("this door
   182	  is closed on purpose"); the range; where the raw test and battery output lies. Never the lead's reasoning, a
   183	  summary, "tests are green", or another auditor's verdict. Its first question: *does this do what he
   184	  asked?*
   185	- **Its hand:** Sol queries the construction engine itself through its one tool `sql_read`
   186	  (`scripts/governance/sol-db-mcp.mjs`, role `sol_reader`) — the lead never hands over a count Sol can
   187	  take. Its sandbox has no network, so the lead runs the database-writing tests and hands their output
   188	  over whole.
   189	- **Its findings**, per claim: Claim · Verdict (REFUTED | STANDS AFTER ATTEMPTED REFUTATION |
   190	  UNVERIFIED) · Finding (`file:line`, scenario, impact) · Evidence · Correction required.
   191	  **A** blocks · **B** is fixed in this pass · **C** is older than this work and stays in the job's
   192	  report — never the board; a row opens only on his word.
   193	- **Fix:** a fork or a `helper-writer` fixes each A and B; the lead verifies the finding's own case.
   194	  There is no second Sol round <!-- CEO-OK: sol-single-pass-fixes-by-helper-2026-10-03 -->.
   195	- **Dispute:** the lead runs the evidence Sol gave — it fails → real, fixed; it holds → the finding drops
   196	  and the report says why. A question no test can settle is the lead's, with its reason in the report.
   197	- **Sol unavailable** (quota, error, timeout): on an ordinary job the `refuter` subagent (Opus 5.5 ·
   198	  `high`) audits once, and the report says *"audited by Opus instead of Sol — read-only by word, not by
   199	  tool — because …"*; on money, a database or security the lead waits for Sol's window and works on
   200	  something else. No job closes unaudited; the auditor is never swapped silently
   201	  <!-- CEO-OK: fallback-auditor-named-and-refuter-second-round-gone-2026-10-04 -->.
   202	
   203	## 5. Tests
   204	
   205	- New behaviour is proven by a test written first — red before the code, green after. A test that can
   206	  never go red — one that recomputes its expected value the way the code does — is a defect.
   207	- After each piece, its own tests. After Sol's fixes, the touched tests again.
   208	- **Code changed → `pnpm typecheck`, then the battery, before Sol** — Sol reads its output instead of
   209	  marking it unverified, and reads the engine only after the battery has stopped writing to it
   210	  <!-- CEO-OK: dxb-team2-overall-look-fixes-2026-10-06 -->; again after the fixes when a fix changed code;
   211	  and a resident service whose
   212	  code changed is restarted (`dxb-verify`). The typecheck covers `packages/` and `apps/`, then `tests/` and
   213	  `db/seed/` (`tsconfig.tests.json`, since 2026-10-06). The battery: `pnpm construction:battery`, the whole suite in
   214	  both halves against the construction's own engine (port 54422), 1,486 tests, about 6 minutes
   215	  (2026-10-04). Launch it detached from a shell whose `oom_score_adj` is 100; green is the line
   216	  `BATTERY_GREEN`. It answers the one question no single test can: did this break anything else.
   217	- **Text only** → no battery. A door, the core or a record: the commit hook's rulers read those on
   218	  every commit. A persona's words: `bash scripts/persona-ruler.sh` (dxb-verify), which the commit
   219	  hook does not run.
   220	- The commit hook runs by itself on every commit: the secret scan (gitleaks); the records ruler when
   221	  STATE, the board or the ledger change; the opening ruler when the core, a skill or the opening hook
   222	  change; the research ruler and `tests/b46` when the research engine changes.
   223	
   224	## 6. Commit, tell, record
   225	
   226	- **Commit:** one per job, or one per independent piece; the message says what changed and why.
   227	- **The row:** a job that advances a board row rewrites that row in the same commit — what finished becomes
   228	  one dated note, `node scripts/board/note-done.mjs <ID> "<what finished>"`, never a story; what remains open
   229	  stays; the row closes only when all of it is finished (`dxb-close-row`)
   230	  <!-- CEO-OK: rows-keep-up-note-not-story-2026-10-09 -->. The commit's subject names the row in its scope —
   231	  `type(B51 …): …` — and the records ruler's R6 stops the STATE commit while a row is behind work so named. A row left behind by
   232	  another job's commit gets its note from that commit and its folder — measured, never guessed.
   233	- **Tell him** in his language, in a few lines, under three headings — *Sizi bekleyen · Değişen ·
   234	  Bulunan* <!-- CEO-OK: job-report-three-headings-2026-10-03 -->: what waits on him; what changed;
   235	  what was found outside the job, each with a recommendation. An empty heading is not written; a
   236	  defect fixed inside the job is not listed; the proof stays in the work until he asks.
   237	- **His words** are written once, in `scripts/governance/ceo-approvals.json`, and only where a record
   238	  must rest on his order or approval (LAW B) — never copied into commits, doors or STATE.
   239	- **STATE** is written once, when the job is told and nothing is left in hand (a session's end cannot
   240	  be seen coming): the three headings, a few lines each — what
   241	  finished and waits for his eye, what is next, what waits on him. No conversation, no quotes, no
   242	  hashes or counts beyond the one that names a job.
   243	- **The job folder** (`.planning/quick/<date>-<slug>/`) holds Sol's report and the raw evidence — only
   244	  for a job that went to Sol. Sol's raw dump (`*.raw`) and its brief (`*brief*.txt`, his words verbatim)
   245	  stay on the disk and out of git (`.gitignore`), and leave the disk after 90 days
   246	  (`scripts/governance/sol-dump-sweep.sh`, run after every commit)
   247	  <!-- CEO-OK: sol-dumps-swept-after-90-days-2026-10-06 -->; `SOL.md`, the verdict, is committed
   248	  <!-- CEO-OK: dxb-team2-overall-look-fixes-2026-10-06 -->.
   249	
   250	## 7. Long commands, context and handover
   251	
   252	- **The 4-minute rules** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-cost-gate.py`): a command over 4 minutes is the
   253	  lead's; a subagent idle over 4 minutes is never resumed — its cache is the 5-minute one.
   254	- **The context gate** (held by `~/.claude/hooks/dxb-context-gate.py`) and **the clean break**: from
   255	  50 % used, hand over at the first clean break — the piece finished well, the tree committed, no
   256	  battery running, the report sent — even when reaching it takes the session to about 60 %; never in
   257	  the middle of a piece. The clean break and the quality decide, not the number; the lead judges it,
   258	  helpers included <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-the-clean-break-2026-10-06 -->. At 60 % the gate refuses
   259	  the Agent tool — a safety net, past which nothing new starts.
   260	- **The handover** is done by the engineer, never the CEO
   261	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-at-50-without-asking-2026-10-01 -->. A note in the scratchpad, in the author's
   262	  own voice, whose first line tells the successor to open this door — `Skill("dxb-team2")` — before
   263	  anything else: it carries the job on as its lead, the orchestrator, not as a fresh session
   264	  <!-- CEO-OK: successor-opens-dxb-team2-2026-10-09 -->; then the job's place in the whole, then his words of this job verbatim, every phase with
   265	  status and commit, the traps met, the first message the successor sends; session-only orders marked
   266	  as such; last, the path of this session's own conversation —
   267	  `~/.claude/projects/-home-dxb-DxB-Global-OS/$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID.jsonl` — with the line *if
   268	  anything here is unclear, read the part you need there*
   269	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-carries-transcript-2026-10-03 -->, `wl-copy`'d. He is quoted verbatim — from
   270	  the ledger, or from this conversation marked so
   271	  <!-- CEO-OK: handover-quotes-conversation-verbatim-2026-10-03 --> — never composed in his first
   272	  person. The successor opens with the same model at his saved default effort, in a VS Code
   273	  editor-area terminal, through `operator` (look first; if the CEO is typing, wait):
   274	  `operator key ctrl+shift+p` → `operator type "Terminal: Create New Terminal in Editor Area"` →
   275	  `operator key Return` → `operator shot` → paste with `operator key ctrl+shift+v` (`ctrl+v` does not
   276	  reach the terminal; `operator type` inverts case here, so a command line is pasted, never typed) the
   277	  line
   278	  `systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --collect -p MemoryMax=16G -p MemorySwapMax=4G -- /home/dxb/.local/bin/claude --model <same> "$(cat <note>)"`
   279	  (`claude` is not on that terminal's PATH) → `operator shot`, read it → `operator key Return`.
   280	  Proof it is alive: `ListAgents` shows it and it answers. If the job is in its plan phase, the
   281	  successor's first reply carries the max warning (§2).

