     9	---
    10	
    11	## 1. Why this exists
    12	
    13	The holding already forbids an agent from approving its own critical work: the
    14	runtime council writes with one model and *refutes* with another
    15	(`packages/orchestrator/src/critical-gate.ts:4` — "Opus 5 WRITES the answer.
    16	Challengers try to REFUTE it", challengers `gpt-5.6-sol` and `gpt-5.5`,
    17	line 125 "another model owns authorship").
    18	
    19	The builder was exempt from his own rule. Measured 2026-07-26: Standing Order 11
    20	excluded subagents from construction entirely, so every "done" claim in this repo
    21	rested on machine gates plus the author's own word. The machine gates are strong
    22	and they catch code defects — 632 tests, `tsc -b`, the DB suite, the E2E tier,
    23	`scripts/i18n-purity-check.sh`, the RULE #0 pass. They do not catch **claim
    24	defects**: "this is finished" when it is not, "this was verified" when the
    25	verification measured something else, a record that no longer matches reality.
    26	
    27	Every defect that has reached the CEO's eye belongs to that class — the ellipsis
    28	truncation, the English cards on the Turkish board, the dead chat, the U28
    29	record-versus-reality mismatch, a RULE #0 pass that reported PASS while
    30	photographing the login page. The audit twin exists for exactly that class, and
    31	for nothing else.
    32	
    33	**The principle, in one line: the rule the holding imposes on its agents applies
    34	to the one who builds them.**
    35	
    36	---
    37	
    38	## 2. The line that must never move
    39	
    40	| | Allowed | Forbidden |
    41	|---|---|---|
    42	| **Authorship** | The session's authorized author (the model that runs the session) writes every repo line **inline** | A subagent writing, editing, or generating any repo content — code, migration, spec, test, commit message. K1 is untouched. |
    43	| **Audit** | An independent agent given a *claim* and its *evidence pointers*, whose only job is to **refute** | An agent asked "does this look good?", or asked to approve, sign off, or close a row |
    44	| **Breadth** | Read-only sweeps that return a table ("which routes are still single-language", "which tables have no RLS") | Any sweep that edits, seeds, migrates, restarts, or otherwise mutates |
    45	| **Verdict** | The session author owns every verdict and every ✓ | A subagent's finding is EVIDENCE, never a verdict. No row closes on a subagent's word. |
    46	
    47	A subagent that writes is a governance violation of the same tier as a silent
    48	deviation (RET + recorded). This directive widens nothing about authorship; it
    49	only removes the exemption the author had from adversarial review.
    50	
    51	---
    52	
    53	## 3. Read-only is MECHANICAL, not promised
    54	
    55	An auditor that promises not to write is not read-only. Enforcement is by tool:
    56	
    57	- **Codex lane (cross-model, the critical gate):** `codex exec -s read-only`
    58	  — the sandbox refuses writes; this is the same mechanism the runtime council
    59	  already runs under (`critical-gate.ts:161` `codexRunner`). The working
    60	  directory is the repo for an audit (the council's own runner uses a temp dir;
    61	  pointing it at the repo is the only adaptation needed).
    62	- **Claude lane (breadth sweeps):** tool set restricted to `Read`, `Grep`,
    63	  `Glob`. No `Edit`, no `Write`, no `NotebookEdit`.
    64	- **When a measurement genuinely needs a command** (a `psql` count, a `git log`),
    65	  the rule is not "no commands" — an auditor that cannot measure is theatre. The
    66	  rule is **no writing commands**: no `INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE`, no migrations, no
    67	  service restarts, and **never the test suites** — they write (today to the
    68	  construction engine, port 54422; once, before it existed, 3,087 false
    69	  `tool_missing` rows into the live audit table). The author runs them and hands
    70	  the auditor the whole raw output; the Codex sandbox has no network anyway.
    71	- **The auditor's own hand into the construction engine** (dxb-team2 job 1,
    72	  CEO 2026-09-28 *"önerin tmm"*, plan approved *"Tamam onaylıyorum."*): the
    73	  Codex lane reaches the database through ONE MCP tool, `sql_read`
    74	  (`scripts/governance/sol-db-mcp.mjs`), as the role `sol_reader`
    75	  (`scripts/governance/sol-reader-role.sh`) — SELECT in four schemas, no write
    76	  verb, sequence, TEMP, CREATE or effectful function anywhere, every statement
    77	  one read inside `BEGIN READ ONLY`, the engine's identity triple checked on the
    78	  connection that runs it. It exists on the construction engine only; the
    79	  company's is refused by identity. So the auditor counts and reads rows itself
    80	  instead of receiving them from the author; the test suites are still the
    81	  author's to run. The profile it runs under is tracked
    82	  (`scripts/governance/codex-refuter.config.toml`) and every other hand that
    83	  runs outside the sandbox — plugins, browser, computer, web, memory, a Node
    84	  REPL — is off, measured by `refuter.sh` on every launch.
    85	- **Proof, not trust:** the author records row counts of `audit_log`, `tasks`,
    86	  `opportunities` and `agent_runs` **before and after** every audit. A
    87	  difference invalidates the audit and is recorded as a defect of the audit
    88	  itself.
