    90	---
    91	
    92	## 4. When the twin fires — on every construction job, at the job's depth
    93	
    94	The CEO's word, 2026-09-28 <!-- CEO-OK: audit-twin-every-job-2026-09-28 -->:
    95	*"bir iş yapıldığında yapan kişi kendisini mi denetliyor bu ii mi?"* — no. The
    96	author also writes the tests and the done-list, so his blind spot and his tests'
    97	blind spot are the same one; the pilot below proved it (a fake-metric page, an
    98	automatic RET, passed six months of machine gates and fell to the twin's first
    99	run). **The author never approves his own work.** Every construction job
   100	(`.claude/skills/dxb-team2`) passes one independent auditor, its depth set by the
   101	job's score card: light → Sol `medium`, normal → Sol `high`, critical → Sol
   102	`xhigh`. It still fires, as before, on:
   103	
   104	1. **The CEO acceptance session** (`ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA.md`, roadmap W5.1) —
   105	   cross-model, because a Claude auditing a Claude shares its blind spots.
   106	2. **A row closing whose evidence has a leg the machine cannot check** — a claim
   107	   about completeness, about a record matching reality, about "nobody has to do
   108	   this by hand any more".
   109	3. **A defect the CEO caught** — the auditor sweeps that defect's **class**
   110	   across the whole repo, not the one instance.
   111	
   112	**One audit per job, not per commit, not per run.** What keeps it from being
   113	ritual is its depth, not its absence.
   114	
   115	---
   116	
   117	## 5. The auditor's contract
   118	
   119	**Input** — the claim, and where to measure it. Never the author's summary,
   120	never his conclusion. A file path, a command, a table name; the auditor forms its
   121	own opinion from the same ground the author stood on.
   122	
   123	**Task** — *refute*. Not review, not improve, not rewrite. Written in the prompt
   124	in those words, the same way the runtime gate words it.
   125	
   126	**Output** — one row per claim:
   127	
   128	| claim | verdict | the command that was run | its decisive output |
   129	|---|---|---|---|
   130	
   131	- `REFUTED` — with the measurement that breaks it.
   132	- `STANDS` — **only** with the commands that failed to break it. "Looks fine" is
   133	  not an output; a silent audit is an invalid audit.
   134	- `UNMEASURABLE` — the honest third answer, with the reason.
   135	
   136	**Disagreement** — the auditor refutes, the author defends, neither can prove:
   137	the claim **cannot stay ✓**. It drops to `⚠ UNVERIFIED` in the report and in the
   138	record. Silence in favour of the author is forbidden.
   139	
   140	**Sequence** — the audit runs **after** the author's full battery, never instead
   141	of it. A machine-catchable defect that the auditor finds is recorded as the
   142	**author's** defect, not as an audit success. Otherwise the checklist quietly
   143	degrades into "the auditor will catch it".
   144	
   145	---
   146	
   147	## 6. Pilot measurement (written before the first run, W5.1)
   148	
   149	| Metric | Threshold |
   150	|---|---|
   151	| Criteria that received a command-backed refutation attempt | ≥ 24 / 27 |
   152	| Findings supported by a command / total findings | ≥ 80% |
   153	| False alarms (finding disproved by measurement) | ≤ 25% |
   154	| Write-check: row counts before vs after | identical, exactly |
   155	| Wall-clock + token cost | recorded (no measurement exists yet — the pilot produces the first) |
   156	
   157	**Stop rule.** If the false-alarm rate exceeds 25%, or the audit surfaces
   158	nothing the batteries did not already have, the twin is withdrawn. It stays
   159	because it works, not because it is written down.
   160	
   161	**Cost, honestly.** Measured basis: `cost_ledger` carries 3.59 billion tokens
   162	over 30 days at €0.0000 — construction and runtime both ride subscription lanes,
   163	so the OS's metered €50-150 budget is untouched by this. Estimated cost to
   164	project completion: ~10 audit runs, ~1M tokens total, roughly 0.03% of current
   165	volume; the real currency is subscription headroom and wall-clock (5-15 minutes
   166	per audit), not euros. Everything in this paragraph after the first sentence is
   167	an ESTIMATE and is labelled as one until the pilot measures it.
   168	
   169	---
   170	
   171	## 7. What this does NOT do
   172	
   173	- It does not raise the quality ceiling. It **lowers variance** — it removes the
   174	  worst days, not improves the best ones.
   175	- It does not delegate the CEO's eye. `ACCEPTANCE_CRITERIA.md` marks 11 of 27
   176	  criteria as tier **C**, explicitly *"hiçbir modele devredilemez"*. On those
   177	  rows the auditor's only legitimate job is to confirm that **nobody claimed
   178	  them as verified**.
   179	- It does not create authority. A subagent cannot close a row, cannot approve, and
   180	  cannot sign. The author signs; the record shows what was attacked and what
   181	  survived.
   182	
   183	---
   184	
   185	## 8. Pilot result — run 1 (2026-07-26 23:00-23:50, W5.1 pre-acceptance)
   186	
   187	| Metric | Threshold | Measured | Verdict |
   188	|---|---|---|---|
   189	| Criteria given a command-backed refutation attempt | ≥ 24 / 27 | **19 / 27** (8 UNMEASURABLE: 6 tier-C rows correctly refused as never-delegable, 2 blocked because the sandbox cannot reach the database container) | **MISSED** — and the reason is the author's prompt, not the auditor: the Codex sandbox has no database, which was foreseeable and unaddressed |
   190	| Findings backed by a command | ≥ 80% | **14 / 14 = 100%** (every block carried the command it ran) | MET |
   191	| False alarms | ≤ 25% | **3 / 14 = 21%** (RLS "gap" was fail-closed by design — zero grants; "Command Center" is the wordmark, not untranslated copy; the org-graph row named the wrong view in the DOC, the product reads the right one) | MET |
   192	| Write-check (`audit_log`/`tasks`/`agent_runs`/`opportunities`) | identical | **26753/216/377/5 → 26753/216/377/5** | MET |
   193	| Cost | record it | 4 agents, ~250k tokens, ~50 min wall-clock, €0 metered (subscription lanes) | recorded |
   194	
   195	**What it caught that the batteries did not** — three confirmed defects, all fixed in the same turn:
   196	
   197	1. **§35 violation live in the product.** `/design-preview` shipped in the production build and rendered invented metrics ("Active agents 24", "Daily cost 6.80 EUR", an agent table with 128 runs at €4.12), and `/design-audit` linked to it from the command side. §35 makes fake metric data an automatic RET. Now dev-only (404 in production) with an E2E gate.
   198	2. **The risk register was half-translated.** `project_risks` had no `title_tr`/`note_tr`, so /gov/risks and every project's Command View showed English sentences on the Turkish board. Migrations 20260726017000 + 017500, both consumers localised, purity gate extended.
   199	3. **The acceptance record contradicted the roadmap.** This file said "no session yet" while `IMPLEMENTATION_ROADMAP.md:160` carried a verbatim CEO approval from 2026-07-18. U28 class. Reconciled above.
   200	
   201	**And one the CEO caught while the audit ran** — seven "Başarısız" rows on his Görevler board were test probes from the e8 observability suites, left behind when a suite failed mid-run. The global teardown now sweeps that class (fourth one it owns). That is a defect of the author, not of the audit — the pilot's own rule.
   202	
   203	**Author-side lessons recorded:** the Codex lane needs the prompt on **stdin** (a positional argument makes `codex exec` wait for stdin and burn the whole timeout); the breadth sweep found no dummy data because the author scoped it to `(command)` pages while the fake-metric route lived outside that group — a narrow scope produces a clean answer that means nothing.
   204	
   205	**Verdict on the twin itself: it earns its place.** It found a §35 automatic-RET defect on a shipped surface that six months of machine gates never flagged, at a false-alarm rate inside the threshold and with a clean write-check. The missed coverage metric is fixable (give the auditor database reach, or split the DB-dependent rows to a lane that has it) and is scheduled for run 2, before the CEO's acceptance session.
     1	# CEO DIRECTIVE — PERFECTION GATE (2026-07-17)
     2	
     3	> **Tier: SEVEREST** — same enforcement class as RULE #0 (Design Verification)
     4	> and RULE #0-A (Measure, Never Guess). Binds every author (Opus 5,
     5	> GPT 5.6, runtime agents) on every deliverable until project end.
     6	> Source: CEO complaint document (Desktop `şikayet.odt`, 2026-07-17) —
     7	> "HER YAPTIĞINIZ İŞE BAKIP BU MÜKEMMEL Mİ MANTIKLI MI DAHA İYİSİ OLUR MU
     8	> DİYE SORACAKSINIZ. BUNU KANUNLAŞTIR."
     9	
    10	## The Law
    11	
    12	**No deliverable ships until its author has explicitly answered three
    13	questions about it — and acted on the answers:**
    14	
    15	1. **Is this PERFECT?** — Not "does it compile", not "does the test pass".
    16	   Would a world-class specialist in this exact craft sign it with their
    17	   name? If any part is embarrassing under that gaze, it is not done.
    18	2. **Is this LOGICAL?** — Does the shape of the thing match the shape of
    19	   the problem? A department page that dumps every employee, a voice line
    20	   that speaks the wrong language, an input that hides where intent goes —
    21	   each "worked" and each was illogical. Structure must serve the user's
    22	   mental model, not the implementer's convenience.
    23	3. **Could it be BETTER?** — Name the concrete better version. If it is
    24	   reachable within the current scope, build it now. If it belongs to a
    25	   future roadmap row, record it as a boundary (00-INDEX U-table or the
    26	   roadmap row's open-items list) — silently shipping the lesser version
    27	   is a violation.
    28	
    29	## Enforcement
    30	
    31	- The three answers are part of the evidence contract: a closure report
    32	  (SUMMARY, roadmap ✓, STATE update) that cannot show the pass happened
    33	  is incomplete — same standing as a missing Design Verification Pass.
    34	- "It satisfies the spec row" is NOT a defense. The spec is the floor.
    35	  This directive makes quality the ceiling-check on top of every floor.
    36	- Applies to the holding's deliverables — a piece he will use, such as the
    37	  dashboard's CRM section — held to the Ferrari standard of `.claude/CLAUDE.md`,
    38	  a first-place candidate in a world competition; not to every small job (a
    39	  fix, a record, a one-line change). His word of 2026-09-24.
    40	  <!-- CEO-OK: h6-perfection-gate-for-deliverables-2026-09-24 --> The CEO
    41	  seeing sloppy work in a deliverable that the author never questioned =
    42	  governance violation (RET + recorded), regardless of whether the spec
    43	  technically allowed it.
    44	- Pairs with the existing laws: RULE #0 verifies the eye, RULE #0-A
    45	  verifies the facts, PERFECTION GATE verifies the judgment.
    46	
    47	## Recorded trigger cases (why this law exists)
    48	
    49	| Case | What shipped | What the gate would have caught |
    50	|---|---|---|
    51	| Command palette | Intent lane hidden behind a 3-char typing rule; single-line input | "Where does the CEO write a full intent?" has no visible answer |
    52	| /org/hr | Equipment dump of all employees as the "HR department" page | An HR module with zero categorization is not a digital HR |
    53	| Voice line | UI locale forced into STT; TR speech transcribed as EN; EN text spoken by TR phoneme voice | One listen: the output language is unintelligible |
    54	| Voice latency | opus-4.8 routed for 2-4 spoken sentences | "Is a frontier model logical for a voice ack?" — no |

